# tools/color_describe_witness.rish -- the plain human description of ANY color proves itself over the open color algebra (Season G).
#
# Season G (Open Media Primitives) -- the crest of the color-naming family: it composes the tone reader
# (image/color_tone.rye) and the hue-family classifier (image/color_name_group.rye) into the phrase a human says,
# beside the nearest-name finder (image/color_nearest.rye), the named-color table (image/color_names.rye), and the open
# color algebra (image/color.rye).
# Teacher, thanked clean-room: gratitude/w3c-css-color.md (the W3C CSS Color spec + HSL model -- public standards only).
#
# The crux this witness proves is composition without braiding: given ANY color, describe joins its coarse tone and its
# coarse family into one plain phrase -- "dark blue", "light green", a bare "red" for a mid-tone color -- following three
# rules of English color speech (a mid tone stays unspoken; black/white read their tone-word alone; every other case
# joins with a space). describe invents no color fact; tone_of and group_of each decide their own word. Bounded (the
# widest phrase, "light magenta", is thirteen bytes; a narrow buffer refuses by name). No new math, no parser, no
# network, no key, no funds.
#
# Run from the repository root:
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/color_describe_witness.rish

say "color-describe: Language EN -- Season G's color describer; a keeper reads ANY color as one plain human phrase, 'dark blue' or 'light green' or a bare 'red', composed from its tone and its family."
say "color-describe: Style Radiant -- compose not braid; tone_of decides the tone and group_of the family, and this module only JOINS their two words the way a person speaks."
say "color-describe: Lens TAME -- the phrase is bounded by construction (thirteen bytes, the widest); a buffer too narrow refuses by name rather than truncating; deterministic; no network, no key, no funds."

let zig = "vendor/zig-toolchain/zig"
let bin = "image/bin/color_describe"

let build = run ["sh" "-c" "mkdir -p image/bin && env RYE_ZIG=${zig} rye/bin/rye build image/color_describe.rye -femit-bin=${bin}"]
assert build.ok else "color-describe: build failed -- ${build.err}"

let selftest = run ["sh" "-c" "${bin} selftest 2>&1"]
assert selftest.ok else "color-describe: selftest exited non-zero -- ${selftest.out}"

let out = trim selftest.out
say "color-describe: ${out}"

# Honour the loom REDS #76 named: check every claim with Rishi's native `contains`, never a shell grep over interpolated
# output, so punctuation in the selftest prose can never break a check.
assert out contains "GREEN image-color-describe" else "color-describe: selftest did not print the GREEN color-describe line"
assert out contains "leaves its tone unspoken" else "color-describe: a mid tone did not drop its word"
assert out contains "joins with a space" else "color-describe: a departing tone did not join the family with a space"
assert out contains "the widest phrase writes whole" else "color-describe: the widest phrase did not write to its bound"
assert out contains "reads its tone-word alone" else "color-describe: a black or white family did not read tone-alone"
assert out contains "the phrase is exactly tone_of plus family" else "color-describe: the phrase drifted from its composed parts"
assert out contains "refuses DescribeBufferTooSmall" else "color-describe: a narrow buffer did not refuse by name"
assert out contains "reads the same phrase every time" else "color-describe: the reading was not deterministic"

say "color-describe: GREEN -- the plain human color description whole over the open color algebra; the three rules of color speech proven, the widest phrase written to its bound, a narrow buffer refused by name, the phrase composed exactly from tone and family, deterministic; the color-naming family reads a color the way a person says it."
